# Normalize every text file to LF in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# Without this, git's `core.autocrlf` (on by default on Windows) hands Windows
# contributors and windows-latest CI runners a CRLF checkout. That breaks two
# classes of thing here:
#
#   1. Generated artifacts. `docs/errors.md`, `grammar/wd-directives.gbnf`, and
#      the VS Code grammar are emitted with LF by their generators, then
#      compared byte-for-byte against the committed copy by drift-guard tests.
#      A CRLF checkout fails those comparisons with a diff that looks identical.
#   2. Docs snippet tests, which read README.md / the docs page off disk and
#      search for LF-shaped `.wd` fragments.
#
# `eol=lf` (not just `text=auto`) is what pins the WORKING TREE, so this holds
# on Windows rather than only inside the object database.
#
# Note this is about THIS repository's own consistency. Darkmown itself accepts
# CRLF source files from users: the compiler normalizes at the reader boundary
# and in parseFrontmatter, guarded by tests/frontmatter.test.js.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be touched by eol conversion.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.vsix binary
*.tgz binary
